WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.940
Secure API design in C++ starts by treating interfaces as security boundaries.

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:13.560
A function, class, plugin hook, callback, or component boundary can shape behavior across many future call sites.

3
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.000
Make contracts explicit.

4
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:25.620
Define caller responsibilities, callee guarantees, accepted input, rejected input, ownership, lifetime, authority, and failure behavior.

5
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.100
Use expressive types to prevent common misuse.

6
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:38.000
Strong domain types, scoped enums, clear optional values, and result types make important assumptions visible at compile time and review time.

7
00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:46.360
Validate input before trust, convert accepted data into narrow internal forms, and preserve invariants during failure.

8
00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.220
Keep diagnostics useful while avoiding sensitive leakage.

9
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.660
Limit authority with scoped contexts or capability-style handles.

10
00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:59.160
Make powerful operations explicit and hard to call accidentally.

11
00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:07.040
Finally, version APIs deliberately, test invalid and denied cases, and review interface changes as security-critical.

12
00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:12.100
A small API decision can influence thousands of maintenance decisions over time.

13
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:16.220
Keep unsafe shortcuts rare, named, and restricted.

14
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:25.200
If an operation is meant only for migration, testing, or privileged maintenance, the interface should make that context obvious.

15
00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:29.400
Secure API design is ultimately about reducing surprise.

16
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:37.260
Callers, reviewers, testers, and operators should all be able to see what the boundary trusts and what it refuses.

17
00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:43.300
That visibility is what lets C++ systems stay maintainable as teams and call sites grow.
